::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A translation maps triangle ABC exactly onto triangle DEF. What conclusion is supported? {
= Triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF#Correct. Triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Triangle ABC is similar but not congruent to triangle DEF#This choice reflects a misconception: Triangle ABC is similar but not congruent to triangle DEF. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Triangle ABC has larger side lengths than triangle DEF#This choice reflects a misconception: Triangle ABC has larger side lengths than triangle DEF. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ No conclusion can be made from a translation#This choice reflects a misconception: No conclusion can be made from a translation. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation can be used to justify congruence? {
~ Dilation with scale factor 3#This choice reflects a misconception: Dilation with scale factor 3. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Translation 4 units right#Correct. Translation 4 units right matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Vertical stretch by factor 2#This choice reflects a misconception: Vertical stretch by factor 2. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Horizontal compression#This choice reflects a misconception: Horizontal compression. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A reflection maps quadrilateral JKLM onto quadrilateral PQRS. Why are the figures congruent? {
= Reflection preserves distance and angle measure#Correct. Reflection preserves distance and angle measure matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Reflection changes every side length#This choice reflects a misconception: Reflection changes every side length. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Reflection proves only similarity#This choice reflects a misconception: Reflection proves only similarity. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Reflection changes the number of vertices#This choice reflects a misconception: Reflection changes the number of vertices. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the best congruence justification? {
~ The figures look equal in the diagram#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures look equal in the diagram. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= A rotation maps one figure exactly onto the other#Correct. A rotation maps one figure exactly onto the other matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ The figures are near each other on the coordinate plane#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures are near each other on the coordinate plane. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The figures have the same color#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures have the same color. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is reflected and then translated. The final image lands exactly on another figure. What can be concluded? {
= The figures are congruent#Correct. The figures are congruent matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ The figures must be different sizes#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures must be different sizes. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only the angles changed#This choice reflects a misconception: Only the angles changed. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The sequence was non-rigid#This choice reflects a misconception: The sequence was non-rigid. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}
